FILENAME: 010419R_SK_EP1_JP83.XSP
 
PURPOSE OF DELIVERY: Final, archival trajectory reconstruction for Cassini
                     for the period from Earth flyby plus one day to Jupiter
                     plus 83 days.
 
DELIVERED BY: Joan Pojman 3-5177. The original nio file was produced
              by Duane Roth.
 
DELIVERY DATE: April 20, 2001.
 
FILE USAGE RESTRICTIONS: None
 
PLANETARY EPHEMERIS USED: A version of DE405 updated by OD:  peph.upd
 
SATELLITE/ASTEROID/ROCK EPHEMERIS USED:  Jupiter (and 16 satellites): jup171s
 
FILE START EPOCH: 19-AUG-1999 02:00:00.0000 ET
 
FILE END EPOCH: 24-MAR-2001 00:00:00.0000 ET
 
START EPOCH OF RECONSTRUCTED DATA ARC: 19 AUGUST 1999 02:04:30 UTC.
 
END EPOCH OF RECONSTRUCTED DATA ARC: 23 MARCH 2001 04:10:30 UTC.
 
COMMENTS:
 
2 and 3-way Doppler (0.2 mm/s), range (50 m) solution with data through
23 March 2001 04:10 UTC (JP83D). Estimate s/c state, solar pressure, solar
torque, RTG radiation, two finite burns (TCM's 13 and 17), 80 impulsive
delta-V's  (which include TCM 14), Jupiter planet ephemeris, and stochastic
non-gravitational accelerations.  Consider Earth-Moon barycenter ephemeris,
station locations, troposphere dry and wet calibrations, and ionosphere
night and day calibrations.
 
Since the updated planetary ephemeris is applicable only to this
reconstruction, the planetary, satellite, and spacecraft ephemerides have
been merged in file 010420R_SCPSE_EP1_JP83.BSP.  The following bodies and
timeframes can be found in file 010419_SK_EP1_JP83.BSP:
 
 
